<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  Mac App Store entitlements for the NESTED MCP relay bundle
  (Contents/Library/Helpers/allodia-mcp.app, docs/mcp.md), applied by Scripts/package.sh's
  app-store flow. The Developer-ID flow signs the same bundle with NO entitlements: it is not
  sandboxed there and needs none.

  Two keys, and no third. The Store rejects an upload whose nested executables lack
  com.apple.security.app-sandbox (ITMS-90296), and the app group is what lets the sandboxed relay
  connect() to the socket the app binds in the shared container.

  WHY NOT com.apple.security.inherit, which is what Apple's "Embedding a helper tool in a
  sandboxed app" prescribes: that document describes a helper THE APP ITSELF SPAWNS, which
  inherits the app's sandbox. This relay is spawned by the user's assistant, an unrelated,
  unsandboxed process, and Apple is explicit that a non-sandboxed parent launching a child with
  `inherit` is an error, because there is no sandbox to inherit. MEASURED here (2026-08-03):
  SIGTRAP in _libsecinit_appsandbox before main(), so the relay would never start. The
  configuration in this file is the one that was measured to work end to end.

  Adding keys here is not free. Apple's guidance for an embedded tool is that extra entitlements
  cause launch failures, and the two that look useful do nothing for a Unix socket anyway
  (temporary-exception file grants do not cover connect(); network.client is AF_INET only).
-->
<dict>
    <!-- Required on every executable in a Store submission. Without it: ITMS-90296 at upload. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The shared container holding mcp.sock. Byte-identical to the app's own group entitlement
         and to McpEndpoint.appGroupIdentifier; a mismatch is a relay that launches and then
         cannot connect, which reads to a user as "the app is not running".

         No team-id prefix: macOS accepts a `group.`-style identifier (MEASURED 2026-08-03, full
         round trip through ~/Library/Group Containers/group.eu.allodia.mailcal/), and the shorter
         string buys 5 bytes of the sun_path budget, which is the tightest constraint on this
         path. Note this is NOT the same shape as keychain-access-groups next door in the app's
         entitlements, which does take $(AppIdentifierPrefix). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>group.$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
